<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Settings come from Directory.Build.props (§4.4).

    Common.Domain, §4.2's second row, drawn by PR-14 and not before. The edge
    belongs to the first type here that genuinely names a domain type, and
    that is §7.5's IDomainEventCollector — IReadOnlyList&lt;IDomainEvent&gt; in
    a signature. IIntegrationEventMapper, IProjectionRegistry and
    DomainEventDispatcher all name one too, and all four arrive together.

    What did NOT draw it is worth keeping, because the argument still governs
    the two files concerned. Not "TransactionBehavior counts IAggregateRoot",
    which this comment said until a review checked it: §6.3's behaviour reads
    IUnitOfWork.ModifiedAggregateCount — an int — and calls
    IDomainEventDispatcher.DispatchAsync(CancellationToken); neither signature
    names a domain type. The `is IAggregateRoot` test lives in EfUnitOfWork,
    over EF's change tracker, and counting behind the port is the whole point.
    A reference existing is not permission for those two to start using it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- IServiceCollection and ILogger<T>. Packages rather than a framework
         reference: §4.2 keeps ASP.NET Core out of Application, and the shared
         framework is how a host would otherwise acquire both. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <!-- The convention scan behind AddPluggableFrom (§6.2). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Scrutor" />
    <!-- ValidationBehavior's IValidator<T> and ValidationException (§6.3). -->
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Common.Domain\Common.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
